4. The Cognitive State Manifold: Explicit Construction

A central concern identified in peer review was the assumption of a compact cognitive manifold without explicit construction. We resolve this by defining \mathcal{M} as a product of three compact factor spaces:

\mathcal{M} = \mathbb{S}^{d_{\text{sem}}-1} \times [0,1]^{n_{\text{log}}} \times \Delta^{k_{\text{ent}}-1},

where:

· \mathbb{S}^{d_{\text{sem}}-1} is the unit sphere in \mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{sem}}}, hosting semantic embeddings normalised to unit length (cf. Gladstone et al., 2025). The sphere is a compact smooth manifold.
· [0,1]^{n_{\text{log}}} is the hypercube of logical truth values, one dimension per propositional variable; it carries the Gödel relaxations of Boolean constraints. The hypercube is a compact manifold‑with‑corners.
· \Delta^{k_{\text{ent}}-1} is the probability simplex of dimension k_{\text{ent}}-1, representing the predictive distribution over the next reasoning step. The simplex is compact and convex.

All three factors are compact, hence their product is compact by the Tychonoff theorem. We equip \mathcal{M} with the product Riemannian metric

g = g_{\text{sem}} \oplus g_{\text{log}} \oplus g_{\text{ent}},

where g_{\text{sem}} is the round metric on the sphere, g_{\text{log}} is the Euclidean metric restricted to the hypercube, and g_{\text{ent}} is the Fisher information metric on the simplex. This construction provides the compactness required for Theorem 5.1.

Boundary treatment. The hypercube [0,1]^{n_{\text{log}}} has corners where the usual gradient is not defined. We adopt a projected‑gradient approach: after each gradient step in the ambient space, we project back onto the feasible set. The projection map P_{\mathcal{M}} : \mathbb{R}^{n_{\text{log}}} \to [0,1]^{n_{\text{log}}} defined by P_{\mathcal{M}}(\mathbf{x})_i = \min(\max(x_i, 0), 1) is a well‑defined retraction, as established in the Riemannian optimisation literature (Absil & Malick, 2012; He et al., 2025). Alternatively, an interior‑point log‑barrier \phi(\mathbf{x}) = -\sum_i (\log x_i + \log(1-x_i)) may be added to the energy functional to keep variables strictly inside (0,1); the barrier parameter \mu \to 0 recovers the exact constraints.

Metric learning. Following Ale (2025), the metric g may be parameterised by learned parameters \theta. Under the assumption of bounded sectional curvature |\sec| \leq K and Lipschitz dependence of g_\theta on \theta, the stability proofs of Section 6 extend with convergence rates modified by the curvature bound. We return to this extension in Section 11.

