12. Kramers Escape Rates and Hallucination Risk Control

Theorem 12.1 (Kramers Escape on the Cognitive Manifold). For the Langevin dynamics ds = -\nabla E(s) dt + \sqrt{2T} dW_t on \mathcal{M}, let \Omega be the basin of attraction of a local minimum s^*, separated from the rest of the state space by an index‑1 saddle s^\dagger with barrier height \Delta E = E(s^\dagger) - E(s^*). Then as T \to 0:

\mathbb{E}[\tau_{\text{exit}}] \asymp \frac{2\pi}{|\lambda_-(s^\dagger)|} \sqrt{\frac{|\det \operatorname{Hess}_E(s^\dagger)|}{\det \operatorname{Hess}_E(s^*)}} \exp\left(\frac{\Delta E}{T}\right),

where \lambda_-(s^\dagger) < 0 is the single negative Hessian eigenvalue at the saddle.

Proof. Bovier et al. (2004) provide “the first rigorous proof of the classical Eyring–Kramers formula in dimension larger than 1”, using a potential‑theoretic approach that relates metastable exit times to capacities of suitably constructed sets. The result extends to compact Riemannian manifolds by local coordinate patches and the variational characterisation of capacities. ∎

Theorem 12.2 (Hallucination‑Risk Control). The probability of escaping a coherent reasoning basin—interpreted as the hallucination risk—scales as \exp(-\Delta E / T). The barrier height \Delta E is a monotonically increasing function of \lambda_{\text{symb}} and \lambda_{\text{mem}}. Therefore, strengthening the symbolic consistency penalty or the memory anchor directly reduces hallucination risk exponentially.

Proof. From the Kramers formula, \mathbb{P}(\text{escape before time } t) \approx 1 - e^{-t / \mathbb{E}[\tau_{\text{exit}}]}. For t large relative to \mathbb{E}[\tau_{\text{exit}}], this scales as t / \mathbb{E}[\tau_{\text{exit}}] \propto \exp(-\Delta E / T). The barrier height \Delta E depends on E_{\text{symb}} and E_{\text{mem}} because the saddle point s^\dagger must violate logical constraints or depart from the initial memory; increasing the corresponding penalty weights raises the saddle energy, thereby increasing \Delta E. ∎

Theorem 12.3 (Gödel Trick for Escaping Local Minima). Introducing stochastic noise via the Gödel Trick (Daniele & van Krieken, 2026) converts the deterministic local search into a stochastic process that escapes shallow local minima of E_{\text{symb}} with probability proportional to \exp(-\Delta E_{\text{local}} / T). Annealing T \to 0 ensures convergence to a global satisfying assignment with high probability.

Proof. The Gödel Trick reparameterises the logical variables as x_i = \sigma(z_i + \xi_i) where \xi_i is Gumbel noise. This injection of noise enables the gradient dynamics to overcome barriers of height \Delta E_{\text{local}}. The connection to the Gumbel‑Max trick provides formal equivalence to sampling from a Boltzmann distribution over assignments. ∎

