14. Connection to Energy‑Based Transformers

The framework provides a geometric reinterpretation of Energy‑Based Transformers (Gladstone et al., 2025). In EBTs, the internal representations undergo iterative gradient‑based refinement. Within our framework, this corresponds to a discrete gradient flow on a data‑dependent Riemannian manifold, where the EBT’s energy function E_\theta(x, y) defines the potential, and the transformer’s internal layers approximate retractions of the gradient steps.

Proposition 14.1 (EBT–Manifold Correspondence). Let E_\theta(x, y) be the energy function of an EBT. The iterative refinement y^{(k+1)} = y^{(k)} - \eta \nabla_y E_\theta(x, y^{(k)}) can be viewed as the Euler discretisation of the gradient flow on the manifold of predictions with metric g_y = \nabla_y^2 E_\theta(x, y) (the Fisher metric).

Proof. The Hessian \nabla_y^2 E_\theta is positive definite at minima, defining a Riemannian metric. The gradient flow with respect to this metric coincides with the natural gradient descent, which is known to be the continuous‑time limit of mirror descent. ∎

